Q: Why can self-invocation bypass Spring AOP?

Because Spring AOP (default) is **proxy-based**.
Advice runs only when the call goes through the proxy.
`this.method()` is a direct call on the target object → proxy skipped.

────────────────────────────────────────
Example

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    public void processPayment() {
        validatePayment(); // self-invocation → bypasses proxy
    }

    @Transactional
    public void validatePayment() {
        // DB operation
    }
}

External call (works):
Caller → Spring Proxy → @Transactional interceptor → validatePayment()

Internal call (fails for AOP):
Caller → Proxy → processPayment() → this.validatePayment()  // no interceptor

Affected annotations (proxy-based):
@Transactional, @Async, @Cacheable, @Retry, @PreAuthorize, custom @Aspect advice, ...

────────────────────────────────────────
Common solutions

1) Move advised method to another Spring bean (clearest)

@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final ValidationService validationService;

    public void processPayment() {
        validationService.validatePayment(); // goes through ValidationService proxy
    }
}

@Service
public class ValidationService {
    @Transactional
    public void validatePayment() { ... }
}

2) Inject self proxy and call through it (possible, less clean)
3) Use AspectJ weaving mode (true bytecode advice; not default Spring Boot setup)

Also remember: Spring proxy AOP generally needs **public** methods on Spring beans.

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer

“Spring AOP is proxy-based. Self-invocation calls the method via this on the same object, so it never enters the proxy interceptors.
That’s why @Transactional, @Async, @Cacheable, @Retry, etc. can be ignored on internal calls. Fix by calling the method through another Spring bean (or AspectJ).”

